WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.614
Big datasets can feel overwhelming.

2
00:00:02.614 --> 00:00:05.878
The trick is to keep only what you need.

3
00:00:05.878 --> 00:00:10.956
In this lesson, you will meet the pipe, one of the most loved tools

4
00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:14.946
in R, and use select to build a smaller, focused table.

5
00:00:14.946 --> 00:00:16.760
Let's make our data friendlier.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.880
Here are two helpful shortcuts, written as notes.

7
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.855
The pipe operator, percent, greater than, percent, has a shortcut:

8
00:00:25.855 --> 00:00:27.445
Control, Shift and M.

9
00:00:27.445 --> 00:00:31.420
And Shift and Enter helps when a command is long.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.373
The pipe means:

11
00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:41.718
take this, and then do that.

12
00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:43.673
So Dataset, pipe, select, means:

13
00:00:43.673 --> 00:00:46.800
take the Dataset, and then select some columns.

14
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.939
Inside select, I list the columns I want, each in quotes:

15
00:01:02.939 --> 00:01:08.540
province name, district name, sex of household head, marital status, and household size.

16
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.484
Long lines are hard to read.

17
00:01:22.484 --> 00:01:28.195
So I press Enter after each comma, and RStudio keeps the code neatly lined up.

18
00:01:28.195 --> 00:01:30.860
R still reads it as one command.

19
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.875
At the front, I write: demographic, arrow.

20
00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:50.460
So the result is saved as a new data frame, called demographic.

21
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.122
Run it.

22
00:02:04.122 --> 00:02:10.576
In the Environment, demographic appears, with one hundred thirty five rows, and five columns.

23
00:02:10.576 --> 00:02:12.420
Same people, fewer columns.

24
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.668
Click on it, or use View, to open it like a spreadsheet.

25
00:02:17.668 --> 00:02:19.688
Here are our five columns:

26
00:02:19.688 --> 00:02:22.920
province, district, sex, marital status, and household size.

27
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.803
Look how much easier this is to read than the full dataset.

28
00:02:32.803 --> 00:02:37.022
Below in the script, I have also prepared the next steps:

29
00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.940
removing columns, and removing rows.

30
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.309
Take, and then do.

31
00:02:59.309 --> 00:03:00.418
That is the pipe.

32
00:03:00.418 --> 00:03:04.300
Once you get used to it, you will use it every day. Well done!

